---
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '~/components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { site } from '~/data/site';

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'AboutPage',
  name: `About ${site.name}`,
  url: site.siteUrl + site.basePath + '/about/',
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description={`Why ${site.name} exists: a free, private way for Indian students to convert black coaching notes to white A4 PDFs and print them cheaply.`}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="section page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />

      <div class="prose about">
        <span class="eyebrow">About</span>
        <h1>Built by a student, for students.</h1>

        <p>
          Padhle Beta started with a simple frustration. Coaching lecture slides are designed for screens —
          dark backgrounds, white text, slick gradients. They look great in class. They are a disaster
          when you try to print them.
        </p>

        <p>
          One Saturday morning, after burning through a ₹900 ink cartridge printing a 200-page chapter,
          the project began. The first version was a Python script that converted one PDF at a time.
          It was ugly, slow, and only worked on a laptop with Python installed.
        </p>

        <p>
          This site is the same idea, but done properly: entirely in your browser, no upload, no signup,
          no install. You drop a file in, you get a printable file back. That is it.
        </p>

        <h2>What we believe</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Free should mean free.</strong> No "first 3 files free", no watermark on output, no upsell wall. The tools on this site will always be free for the things students actually need.</li>
          <li><strong>Your files are yours.</strong> Every tool runs in your browser. We never see your PDFs, your notes, or your data. We literally could not sell them even if we wanted to.</li>
          <li><strong>Indian students deserve better tooling.</strong> Most PDF software is built for office workers in North America. We are building for the specific problems of NEET/JEE/Boards students who print coaching notes at home.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What's free, and what's coming</h2>

        <p>
          All five core tools are free today and will stay free: dark to light, merge, compress, image to PDF, and extract pages. If the site grows enough to need server-side features (OCR, server-side compression), we may add a paid tier for those specifically. The browser-based tools will not become paid.
        </p>

        <h2>How to support</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Share the site</strong> with friends who are also struggling with ink costs. Word of mouth is the only marketing we do.</li>
          <li><strong>Star us on GitHub</strong> — it helps other students find the project.</li>
          <li><strong>Send feedback.</strong> If a tool is broken, slow, or missing something obvious, we want to know.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Open source</h2>

        <p>
          Every line of code that runs this site is open source and lives at
          <a href={`https://github.com/${site.github.org}/${site.github.repo}`} rel="noopener">
            github.com/{site.github.org}/{site.github.repo}
          </a>.
          Audit it. Fork it. Suggest a pull request. The privacy promise is verifiable because the code is auditable.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about { padding-top: var(--space-5); }
  .about .eyebrow { margin-bottom: var(--space-4); }
  .about h1 { margin-bottom: var(--space-5); }
</style>
